  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A 3D printer builds a solid object by depositing material one thin layer at a time. First, slicing software divides a digital 3D model into a stack of printable layers. For each layer, the slicer calculates the nozzle's path around the outer walls and through the inner structure. The printer heats the nozzle until the plastic filament can flow through its tip. Motors drive the nozzle along the calculated path while it extrudes a thin bead of melted plastic. The deposited plastic cools and solidifies, forming the current layer of your object. The nozzle then rises by one layer height or the bed lowers and the printer repeats the process. Each new layer fuses with the previous one, gradually building a solid shape. So, a 3D printer turns a digital model into an object through repeated extrusion, cooling and layer bonding.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9776598870331514,
